From: EJ Cyr <ejc3@meta.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fs: cache the peer search per group in mountinfo

Generating /proc/<pid>/mountinfo calls get_dominating_id() for every
slave mount. That walks the mount's master chain and, for each master,
scans that master's entire peer ring for a member reachable under the
caller's root. Nothing memoises the scan, so listing N slaves of one
shared tree of P mounts costs O(N * P).

That shape is what a service mount namespace produces. systemd turns the
mounts it inherits into slaves, so on a host whose mounts largely
descend from one shared "/" every line rescans the same large ring.

Measured on a host with 994 mounts, inside a slave namespace holding 985
slave lines across 462 distinct master peer groups. Weighting each line
by the size of the ring it scans gives 258,589 ring steps per listing,
and one group of 508 members accounts for 258,064 of them, or 99.8
percent:

  read /proc/self/mountinfo, root namespace   0.0016 s
  read /proc/self/mountinfo, slave namespace  0.5128 s

Ordinary tools pay it too: df(1) went from 0.0122 s to 0.5414 s between
those namespaces, and mount(8) from 0.0089 s to 0.4919 s.

get_peer_under_root() reads only the ring it is given, plus the
namespace and the root, and both of those are fixed for the duration of
one listing. A ring is identified by the peer group id its members
share, so that id is a valid cache key. Replaying the measured sequence
of master group ids through an 8-entry cache leaves 2,049 of the 258,589
ring steps, a factor of 126; 16 entries gain nothing further.

The key has to be the group id rather than the master pointer. Turning a
shared tree into slaves gives every slave its own master, so the same
run presents 1,859 distinct master pointers naming only 462 rings, and a
pointer-keyed cache would miss almost every time.

Only the first level is cached. Its answer is a property of one ring.
Deeper levels follow each master's own chain, where two mounts sharing a
ring can diverge, so those are walked as before.

The cache cannot go stale: m_start() takes namespace_sem for read and
m_stop() drops it, so the tree is fixed while records are emitted, and
m_start() clears the cache each time it takes the lock.

Signed-off-by: EJ Cyr <ejc3@meta.com>
---
--- a/fs/mount.h
+++ b/fs/mount.h
@@ -138,10 +138,33 @@
 
 extern seqlock_t mount_lock;
 
+/*
+ * Memoises the first-level peer search of get_dominating_id() across one
+ * listing of mountinfo.
+ *
+ * get_peer_under_root() scans the whole peer ring of a mount's master. The
+ * outcome depends only on that ring, the namespace, and the root; the latter
+ * two are fixed for a listing, and a ring is identified by the peer group id
+ * its members share. So the group id is a valid key, and every slave of the
+ * same shared tree answers out of one entry instead of rescanning the ring.
+ *
+ * Keying on the master pointer instead would not work: making a shared tree
+ * into slaves gives each slave its own master, so a tree of N mounts presents
+ * N distinct master pointers that all name the same ring.
+ */
+#define MNT_DOM_CACHE_SIZE 8
+
+struct mnt_dom_cache {
+	int gid[MNT_DOM_CACHE_SIZE];	/* master's peer group id; 0 = unused */
+	bool found[MNT_DOM_CACHE_SIZE];	/* did that ring hold a peer under root */
+	unsigned int victim;		/* next slot to replace */
+};
+
 struct proc_mounts {
 	struct mnt_namespace *ns;
 	struct path root;
 	int (*show)(struct seq_file *, struct vfsmount *);
+	struct mnt_dom_cache dom_cache;
 };
 
 extern const struct seq_operations mounts_op;
--- a/fs/pnode.h
+++ b/fs/pnode.h
@@ -42,6 +42,8 @@
 void propagate_mount_unlock(struct mount *);
 void mnt_release_group_id(struct mount *);
 int get_dominating_id(struct mount *mnt, const struct path *root);
+int get_dominating_id_cached(struct mount *mnt, const struct path *root,
+			     struct mnt_dom_cache *cache);
 int mnt_get_count(struct mount *mnt);
 void mnt_set_mountpoint(struct mount *, struct mountpoint *,
 			struct mount *);
--- a/fs/pnode.c
+++ b/fs/pnode.c
@@ -70,6 +70,55 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * As get_dominating_id(), but memoises the first master's ring scan in cache.
+ *
+ * Only the first level is cached. Its answer is a property of that one ring,
+ * which the group id names. Deeper levels follow each master's own chain, so
+ * two mounts sharing a ring can diverge there, and those levels are walked
+ * uncached. A hit that recorded a peer returns the group id directly: the peer
+ * found is a member of that same ring, so it carries the same id.
+ */
+int get_dominating_id_cached(struct mount *mnt, const struct path *root,
+			     struct mnt_dom_cache *cache)
+{
+	struct mount *first = mnt->mnt_master;
+	struct mount *m, *d;
+	int gid, i;
+
+	if (!first)
+		return 0;
+
+	gid = first->mnt_group_id;
+	if (gid) {
+		for (i = 0; i < MNT_DOM_CACHE_SIZE; i++) {
+			if (cache->gid[i] != gid)
+				continue;
+			if (cache->found[i])
+				return gid;
+			goto deeper;
+		}
+	}
+
+	d = get_peer_under_root(first, mnt->mnt_ns, root);
+	if (gid) {
+		i = cache->victim++ % MNT_DOM_CACHE_SIZE;
+		cache->gid[i] = gid;
+		cache->found[i] = d != NULL;
+	}
+	if (d)
+		return d->mnt_group_id;
+
+deeper:
+	for (m = first->mnt_master; m != NULL; m = m->mnt_master) {
+		d = get_peer_under_root(m, mnt->mnt_ns, root);
+		if (d)
+			return d->mnt_group_id;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int do_make_slave(struct mount *mnt)
 {
 	struct mount *master, *slave_mnt;
--- a/fs/namespace.c
+++ b/fs/namespace.c
@@ -1630,6 +1630,11 @@
 	struct proc_mounts *p = m->private;
 
 	down_read(&namespace_sem);
+	/*
+	 * The mount tree cannot change until m_stop() drops this, so anything
+	 * cached below is valid for exactly this window.
+	 */
+	memset(&p->dom_cache, 0, sizeof(p->dom_cache));
 
 	return mnt_find_id_at(p->ns, *pos);
 }
--- a/fs/proc_namespace.c
+++ b/fs/proc_namespace.c
@@ -160,7 +160,7 @@
 		seq_printf(m, " shared:%i", r->mnt_group_id);
 	if (IS_MNT_SLAVE(r)) {
 		int master = r->mnt_master->mnt_group_id;
-		int dom = get_dominating_id(r, &p->root);
+		int dom = get_dominating_id_cached(r, &p->root, &p->dom_cache);
 		seq_printf(m, " master:%i", master);
 		if (dom && dom != master)
 			seq_printf(m, " propagate_from:%i", dom);
